"Let $n = \\lceil x \\rceil$, where $n$ is an integer. Then $n \\leq x < n+1$.\n\nWe are given that $n + x = \\frac{23}{7}$.\n\nSince $n \\leq x < n+1$, we have $n + x \\leq n + (n+1) = 2n + 1$.\n\nTherefore, $2n + 1 \\leq \\frac{23}{7}$.\n\nSubtracting 1 from both sides, we get $2n \\leq \\frac{16}{7}$.\n\nDividing both sides by 2, we get $n \\leq \\frac{8}{7}$.\n\nSince $n$ is an integer, the only possible value for $n$ is $2$.\n\nSubstituting $n = 2$ into the equation $n + x = \\frac{23}{7}$, we get $2 + x = \\frac{23}{7}$.\n\nSubtracting 2 from both sides, we get $x = \\frac{9}{7}$.\n\nTherefore, $x = \\boxed{\\frac{9}{7}}$."
