"To solve the equation \\( |x-1| = 7 \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The equation \\( |x-1| = 7 \\) means that the distance between \\( x \\) and 1 on the number line is 7. This can be expressed as two separate equations:\n\n1. \\( x - 1 = 7 \\)\n2. \\( x - 1 = -7 \\)\n\nLet's solve each equation separately:\n\n1. \\( x - 1 = 7 \\)\n   \\[\n   x = 7 + 1 = 8\n   \\]\n\n2. \\( x - 1 = -7 \\)\n   \\[\n   x = -7 + 1 = -6\n   \\]\n\nThe solutions to the equation \\( |x-1| = 7 \\) are \\( x = 8 \\) and \\( x = -6 \\). To find the sum of all values of \\( x \\), we add these solutions together:\n\n\\[\n8 + (-6) = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all values of \\( x \\) that satisfy the given equation is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."
"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{4a+3b}{a-2b} = 5\n\\]\n\nFirst, we cross-multiply to eliminate the fraction:\n\\[\n4a + 3b = 5(a - 2b)\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side:\n\\[\n4a + 3b = 5a - 10b\n\\]\n\nRearranging the terms to isolate \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\n4a - 5a = -10b - 3b\n\\]\n\\[\na = -13b\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(a = -13b\\) into the expression \\(\\frac{a+11b}{a-b}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a+11b}{a-b} = \\frac{-13b + 11b}{-13b - b} = \\frac{2b}{-14b} = -\\frac{1}{7}\n\\]\n\nThus, the simplified numerical value of \\(\\frac{a+11b}{a-b}\\) is \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{7}}."
