"To find the $y$-coordinate of the point where the line intersects the $y$-axis, we need to determine the equation of the line passing through the points $(-2, 6)$ and $(-4, -2)$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ of the line:\n\\[\nm = \\frac{-2 - 6}{-4 - (-2)} = \\frac{-8}{-2} = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the point-slope form of the equation of a line, $y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)$, with the point $(-2, 6)$ and slope $m = 4$:\n\\[\ny - 6 = 4(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get:\n\\[\ny - 6 = 4x + 8\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 4x + 14\n\\]\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the line is $y = 14$. Therefore, the $y$-coordinate of the point where the line intersects the $y$-axis is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{14}\n\\]"
