"We start with the given equation \\( ab = a - b \\). Rearranging this equation, we get:\n\n\\[ ab - a + b = 0. \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest possible value of the expression \\( \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{b}{a} - ab \\). Let's denote this expression by \\( S \\):\n\n\\[ S = \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{b}{a} - ab. \\]\n\nWe can rewrite \\( S \\) using the given equation:\n\n\\[ S = \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{b}{a} - (a - b). \\]\n\nSince \\( ab = a - b \\), we substitute \\( a - b \\) with \\( ab \\):\n\n\\[ S = \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{b}{a} - (a - b) = \\frac{a}{b} + \\frac{b}{a} - a + b = 0. \\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible value of \\( S \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{0}. \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(0\\). I hope it is correct."
