"To determine how much greater \\(31^2\\) is compared to \\(19^2\\), we first need to calculate the values of \\(19^2\\) and \\(31^2\\).\n\n1. Calculate \\(19^2\\):\n   \\[\n   19^2 = 19 \\times 19 = 361\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(31^2\\):\n   \\[\n   31^2 = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n   \\]\n\nNext, we find the difference between \\(31^2\\) and \\(19^2\\):\n\\[\n31^2 - 19^2 = 961 - 361\n\\]\n\nPerform the subtraction:\n\\[\n961 - 361 = 600\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(31^2\\) is 600 greater than \\(19^2\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{600}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(600\\). I hope it is correct."
"Given the equation \\(\\lfloor r \\rfloor + r = 15.5\\), we need to determine the number of values of \\(r\\) that satisfy this equation.\n\nLet \\(n = \\lfloor r \\rfloor\\), where \\(n\\) is an integer. Then \\(r = n + f\\), where \\(f\\) is the fractional part of \\(r\\) and \\(0 \\leq f < 1\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(r = n + f\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ n + (n + f) = 15.5 \\]\n\\[ 2n + f = 15.5 \\]\n\\[ f = 15.5 - 2n \\]\n\nSince \\(0 \\leq f < 1\\), we have:\n\\[ 0 \\leq 15.5 - 2n < 1 \\]\n\\[ 14.5 \\leq 2n < 15.5 \\]\n\\[ 7.25 \\leq n < 7.75 \\]\n\nThe integer values of \\(n\\) that satisfy this inequality are \\(7\\). \n\n\\(\\boxed{1}\\)"
