"To find the total number of dollars in the investment at the end of five years, we need to calculate the compound interest over the five years with bi-monthly compounding.\n\nFirst, let's determine the number of bi-monthly periods in five years:\n\\[ 5 \\text{ years} \\times 2 \\text{ periods per year} = 10 \\text{ bi-monthly periods} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the interest rate for each bi-monthly period:\n\\[ 1\\% = 0.01 \\]\n\nThe formula for compound interest is:\n\\[ A = P (1 + r)^n \\]\nwhere \\( P \\) is the principal amount, \\( r \\) is the interest rate per period, and \\( n \\) is the number of periods.\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\\[ A = 24,000 (1 + 0.01)^{10} \\]\n\\[ A = 24,000 \\times 1.1046 \\]\n\\[ A \\approx 26,510 \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $26,510."
