"To find the minimum value of the quadratic expression \\(a^2 + 6a - 7\\), we can complete the square.\n\n1. Start with the expression:\n   \\[\n   a^2 + 6a - 7\n   \\]\n\n2. To complete the square, take half of the coefficient of \\(a\\), square it, and add and subtract this value inside the expression:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{6}{2}\\right)^2 = 3^2 = 9\n   \\]\n   So, we add and subtract 9 inside the expression:\n   \\[\n   a^2 + 6a + 9 - 9 - 7\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   (a + 3)^2 - 16\n   \\]\n\n4. The expression \\((a + 3)^2 - 16\\) is always non-negative and achieves its minimum value of \\(-16\\) when \\(a = -3\\).\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(a^2 + 6a - 7\\) is \\(-16\\)."
"To determine the degree of the polynomial \\((ax^6 + bx^8 + cx^3 + d)(x^5 + ex^4 + f)(x^2 + gx + h)\\), we need to consider the highest degree term in each factor and then sum these degrees.\n\n1. The highest degree term in the first factor \\((ax^6 + bx^8 + cx^3 + d)\\) is \\(ax^8\\). Therefore, the degree of this factor is 8.\n\n2. The highest degree term in the second factor \\((x^5 + ex^4 + f)\\) is \\(x^5\\). Therefore, the degree of this factor is 5.\n\n3. The highest degree term in the third factor \\((x^2 + gx + h)\\) is \\(x^2\\). Therefore, the degree of this factor is 2.\n\nWhen multiplying these polynomials together, the degree of the resulting polynomial is the sum of the degrees of the individual factors. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial is \\(8 + 5 + 2 = 15\\).\n\nThe degree of the polynomial is \\(\\boxed{15}\\)."
