"To find the real root of the equation \\(8x^3 - 3x^2 - 3x - 1 = 0\\), we can use the fact that the polynomial is related to the cube roots of unity. Specifically, the polynomial \\(8x^3 - 3x^2 - 3x - 1\\) is the derivative of \\(x^4 - 1\\) evaluated at \\(x = 1\\).\n\nThe derivative of \\(x^4 - 1\\) is \\(4x^3 - 3x^2\\). Evaluating this at \\(x = 1\\), we get:\n\n\\[4(1)^3 - 3(1)^2 = 4 - 3 = 1.\\]\n\nThus, the real root of the equation is \\(1\\). We can write this in the form \\(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]a + \\sqrt[3]b + 1}{c}\\) with \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 1\\), and \\(c = 1\\).\n\nTherefore, we have \\(a + b + c = 1 + 1 + 1 = \\boxed{3}\\)."
"To find the sum of the squares of the solutions to the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), we can use the formula for the sum of the squares of the roots of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = \\left(-\\frac{b}{a}\\right)^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{c}{a}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFor the given equation \\(2x^2 + 4x - 1 = 0\\), we have \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 4\\), and \\(c = -1\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = \\left(-\\frac{4}{2}\\right)^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{-1}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = (-2)^2 - 2(-1)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sum of squares} = 4 + 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the squares of the solutions is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."
