"To determine the number of ways to form a committee consisting of a chairman and some (not necessarily nonzero) number of members-at-large from a group of 10 candidates, we need to consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Choose the Chairman**: There are 10 candidates to choose from for the position of chairman. Therefore, there are 10 ways to choose the chairman.\n\n2. **Choose the Members-at-Large**: After choosing the chairman, there are 9 remaining candidates. We can choose any number of these candidates to be members-at-large. The number of ways to choose \\( k \\) members-at-large from 9 candidates is given by the binomial coefficient \\( \\binom{9}{k} \\). Since \\( k \\) can be any integer from 0 to 9, there are 10 possible values for \\( k \\).\n\nThus, the total number of ways to form the committee is the product of the number of ways to choose the chairman and the number of ways to choose the members-at-large:\n\n\\[\n10 \\times 10 = 100\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to form the committee is \\(\\boxed{100}\\)."
