"A five-digit palindrome has the form \\( abcba \\), where \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are digits. For the number to be a palindrome, the first and last digits must be the same, and the second and fourth digits must be the same.\n\nGiven the digits 1, 2, and 9, we can determine the possible values for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\):\n- \\( a \\) can be 1, 2, or 9 (3 choices).\n- \\( b \\) can be 1, 2, or 9 (3 choices).\n- \\( c \\) can be 1, 2, or 9 (3 choices).\n\nSince the choices for \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are independent, the total number of five-digit palindromes is the product of the number of choices for each digit:\n\\[ 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 27. \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of five-digit palindromes that can be made from the digits 1, 2, and 9 is \\(\\boxed{243}\\)."
