"To determine the number of different battalions that can be sent, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 4 upper class soldiers out of 5 and 8 lower class soldiers out of 10.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to choose 4 upper class soldiers out of 5:\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{4} = \\frac{5!}{4!(5-4)!} = \\frac{5!}{4! \\cdot 1!} = 5\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 8 lower class soldiers out of 10:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{8} = \\frac{10!}{8!(10-8)!} = \\frac{10!}{8! \\cdot 2!} = 45\n\\]\n\nTo find the total number of different battalions, we multiply the number of ways to choose the upper class soldiers by the number of ways to choose the lower class soldiers:\n\\[\n5 \\times 45 = 225\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different battalions is \\(\\boxed{225}\\)."
