"To find the coefficient of \\(x^3y^3z^2\\) in the expansion of \\((x+y+z)^8\\), we use the multinomial theorem. The multinomial theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x+y+z)^8 = \\sum_{i+j+k=8} \\binom{8}{i!j!k!} x^i y^j z^k\n\\]\n\nWe are interested in the term where \\(i = 3\\), \\(j = 3\\), and \\(k = 2\\). Therefore, we need to calculate:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{3!3!2!} x^3 y^3 z^2\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute the multinomial coefficient:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{8}{3!3!2!} = \\frac{8!}{3!3!2!} = \\frac{40320}{6 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 2} = 56\n\\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of \\(x^3y^3z^2\\) in the expansion of \\((x+y + z)^8\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{56}\n\\]"
"To determine the number of diagonals in a hexagon, we can use the formula for the number of diagonals in an \\( n \\)-sided polygon, which is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of diagonals} = \\frac{n(n-3)}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor a hexagon, \\( n = 6 \\). Plugging \\( n = 6 \\) into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of diagonals} = \\frac{6(6-3)}{2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n6 - 3 = 3\n\\]\n\nSo the formula becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of diagonals} = \\frac{6 \\times 3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, perform the multiplication:\n\n\\[\n6 \\times 3 = 18\n\\]\n\nThen, divide by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{18}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of diagonals in a hexagon is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).\n\nThe final answer is 9. I hope it is correct."
