"To find the distance from point \\( P \\) to point \\( C \\), we can use the distance formula. The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the coordinates of point \\( P \\) and point \\( C \\), we can calculate the distance as follows:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(5 - 0)^2 + (0 - 0)^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{5^2 + 0^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{25}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nd = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance from point \\( P \\) to point \\( C \\) is \\( 5 \\) units.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( 5 \\). I hope it is correct.\n\nNote: The problem asks to express the answer in simplest radical form, but in this case, the answer is already in simplest radical form."
