"To find the ratio of the area of triangle \\(AXY\\) to the area of triangle \\(ABC\\), we can use the concept of similar triangles. Since \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) lie on sides \\(AB\\) and \\(AC\\) respectively, and \\(AX = 4\\) and \\(AY = 6\\), triangle \\(AXY\\) is similar to triangle \\(ABC\\).\n\nThe ratio of the areas of two similar triangles is the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides. Therefore, the ratio of the sides of triangle \\(AXY\\) to triangle \\(ABC\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{AX}{AB} = \\frac{4}{7}\n\\]\n\nThe ratio of the areas of triangle \\(AXY\\) to triangle \\(ABC\\) is then the square of this ratio:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{4}{7} \\right)^2 = \\frac{16}{49}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the area of triangle \\(AXY\\) to the area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{16}{49}}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: \\boxed{\\frac{16}{49}}. I hope it is correct."
"To find the perimeter of the octagon \\(ABCDEFGH\\), we need to sum the lengths of all its sides. The octagon is equiangular, which means all its angles are equal.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(AB = 1\\)\n- \\(BC = 2\\)\n- \\(CD = 3\\)\n- \\(DE = 4\\)\n- \\(EF = FG = 2\\)\n\nSince the octagon is equiangular, all sides are equal. Therefore, \\(GH = EF = 2\\) and \\(HA = FG = 2\\).\n\nNow, we sum the lengths of all the sides:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = AB + BC + CD + DE + EF + FG + GH + HA\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 2 + 2 + 2\n\\]\n\nAdding these together:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 16\n\\]\n\nThus, the perimeter of the octagon is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(16\\)."
