"To factor the expression \\(ab + 5b + 2a + 10\\), we first look for common factors among the terms. Notice that the terms \\(ab\\) and \\(5b\\) have a common factor of \\(b\\), and the terms \\(2a\\) and \\(10\\) have a common factor of \\(2\\).\n\nWe can factor out \\(b\\) from the first two terms:\n\\[ab + 5b = b(a + 5)\\]\n\nWe can factor out \\(2\\) from the last two terms:\n\\[2a + 10 = 2(a + 5)\\]\n\nNow, we can rewrite the original expression as:\n\\[b(a + 5) + 2(a + 5)\\]\n\nWe can factor out the common factor \\((a + 5)\\):\n\\[(a + 5)(b + 2)\\]\n\nTherefore, the factored form of the expression \\(ab + 5b + 2a + 10\\) is \\(\\boxed{(a+5)(b+2)}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{(a+5)(b+2)}.\\)"
