"Since $p(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial with integer coefficients and $3+\\sqrt{7}$ is a root, its conjugate $3-\\sqrt{7}$ must also be a root. Therefore, $p(x)$ can be written as:\n\n$$p(x) = (x - (3+\\sqrt{7}))(x - (3-\\sqrt{7})) = (x - 3)^2 - (\\sqrt{7})^2 = x^2 - 6x + 2 - 7 = x^2 - 6x - 5.$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $\\frac{p(2)}{p(3)}$:\n\n$$p(2) = 2^2 - 6 \\cdot 2 - 5 = 4 - 12 - 5 = -13,$$\n$$p(3) = 3^2 - 6 \\cdot 3 - 5 = 9 - 18 - 5 = -14.$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\\frac{p(2)}{p(3)} = \\frac{-13}{-14} = 1.$$\n\nTherefore, the final answer is $\\boxed{1}$."
"We can use polynomial long division to find the quotient.\n\n1. Divide the first term of the dividend $x^4$ by the first term of the divisor $x^2$ to get $x^2$.\n2. Multiply $x^2$ by $x^2 + 2x - 1$ to get $x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2$.\n3. Subtract $x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2$ from $x^4 + 3x^2 - 7x$ to get $2x^2 - 7x$.\n4. Divide the first term of the new dividend $2x^2$ by the first term of the divisor $x^2$ to get $2$.\n5. Multiply $2$ by $x^2 + 2x - 1$ to get $2x^2 + 4x - 2$.\n6. Subtract $2x^2 + 4x - 2$ from $2x^2 - 7x$ to get $-11x + 2$.\n\nThe quotient is $\\boxed{x^2 + 2}$."
"The given series is a harmonic series where each term is of the form \\(\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\\). We can rewrite each term using partial fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{n(n+1)} = \\frac{1}{n} - \\frac{1}{n+1}\n\\]\n\nThus, the series becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{n=1}^{99} \\left( \\frac{1}{n} - \\frac{1}{n+1} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThis is a telescoping series, where most terms cancel out:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{1} - \\frac{1}{2} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{3} \\right) + \\cdots + \\left( \\frac{1}{99} - \\frac{1}{100} \\right)\n\\]\n\nAll intermediate terms cancel out, leaving:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{1}{100}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the sum is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{99}{100}}\\)."
