"To determine how many different values can be obtained from the expression \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1\\) by inserting parentheses, we need to consider the effect of parentheses on the order of operations.\n\nThe expression \\(2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 5 + 1\\) has four terms: \\(2\\), \\(3\\), \\(4\\), and \\(5\\). When we insert parentheses, we can change the order of operations. Specifically, we can group the terms in different ways.\n\nThe number of ways to group four terms is given by the number of partitions of four numbers. The partitions of four numbers are:\n1. \\(4 + 0\\)\n2. \\(3 + 1\\)\n3. \\(2 + 2\\)\n4. \\(2 + 1 + 1\\)\n5. \\(1 + 1 + 1 + 1\\)\n\nEach partition corresponds to a different way of grouping the terms, which results in a different value for the expression. Therefore, there are 5 different values that can be obtained by inserting parentheses.\n\nThus, the total number of different values is \\(\\boxed{5}\\)."
"To solve the inequality \\(6 - x > -9\\), we need to isolate \\(x\\) on one side of the inequality. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Start with the inequality:\n   \\[\n   6 - x > -9\n   \\]\n\n2. Add 6 to both sides of the inequality:\n   \\[\n   6 - x + 6 > -9 + 6\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   -x > -3\n   \\]\n\n3. Multiply both sides of the inequality by \\(-1\\). Remember that multiplying or dividing both sides of an inequality by a negative number reverses the inequality sign:\n   \\[\n   -1 \\cdot (-x) < -1 \\cdot (-3)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   x < 3\n   \\]\n\nThe inequality \\(x < 3\\) means that \\(x\\) can be any number less than 3. The greatest integer that satisfies this inequality is:\n\\[\nx = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."
