"To simplify \\(\\sqrt{64^3}\\), we can use the properties of square roots and exponents.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite \\(64\\) as a power of \\(2\\):\n\\[ 64 = 2^6 \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(2^6\\) for \\(64\\) in the expression:\n\\[ \\sqrt{64^3} = \\sqrt{(2^6)^3} \\]\n\nNext, use the property of exponents that states \\((a^m)^n = a^{m \\cdot n}\\):\n\\[ (2^6)^3 = 2^{6 \\cdot 3} = 2^{18} \\]\n\nNow, take the square root of \\(2^{18}\\):\n\\[ \\sqrt{2^{18}} = 2^{18/2} = 2^9 \\]\n\nFinally, calculate \\(2^9\\):\n\\[ 2^9 = 512 \\]\n\nTherefore, the simplified form of \\(\\sqrt{64^3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{512}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(512\\)."
"To determine the measure of the angle indicated, we need to understand the geometry of the tessellation formed by three regular pentagons meeting at a vertex.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the interior angle of a regular pentagon. The formula for the interior angle of a polygon with \\( n \\) sides is:\n\\[\n\\text{Interior angle} = \\frac{(n-2) \\times 180^\\circ}{n}\n\\]\nFor a pentagon (\\( n = 5 \\)):\n\\[\n\\text{Interior angle} = \\frac{(5-2) \\times 180^\\circ}{5} = \\frac{3 \\times 180^\\circ}{5} = 108^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWhen three regular pentagons meet at a vertex, the sum of the interior angles at that vertex is:\n\\[\n3 \\times 108^\\circ = 324^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThe exterior angle at the vertex is:\n\\[\n360^\\circ - 324^\\circ = 36^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the measure of the angle indicated is \\(\\boxed{36}\\)."
