"To find the greatest possible quotient of any two distinct members of the set \\(\\{\\frac{2}{5}, \\frac{1}{2}, 5, 10\\}\\), we need to maximize the expression \\(\\frac{x}{y}\\) where \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are chosen from the set.\n\nFirst, let's list the elements of the set:\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{5}, \\frac{1}{2}, 5, 10\n\\]\n\nTo maximize \\(\\frac{x}{y}\\), we should choose the largest element for \\(x\\) and the smallest element for \\(y\\). The largest element in the set is 10, and the smallest element is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nx = 10 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the quotient:\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{10}{\\frac{1}{2}} = 10 \\times 2 = 20\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest possible quotient is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{20}\n\\]"
