"To find the determinant of the matrix \\(\\begin{vmatrix} b & a \\\\ d & c \\end{vmatrix}\\), we can use the property of determinants that states the determinant of a transposed matrix is equal to the determinant of the original matrix. This property is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\det(A) = \\det(A^T)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(A^T\\) is the transpose of \\(A\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{vmatrix} = -8\n\\]\n\nWe need to find:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} b & a \\\\ d & c \\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} b & a \\\\ d & c \\end{vmatrix} = \\begin{vmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{vmatrix}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given value:\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} b & a \\\\ d & c \\end{vmatrix} = -8\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the determinant is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-8}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{-8}\\)."
"The dot product of the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\begin{pmatrix} 12 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}$ is given by:\n\n$$(3)(12) + (-4)(5) = 36 - 20 = 16.$$\n\nThe magnitudes of the vectors are:\n\n$$\\sqrt{3^2 + (-4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5,$$\nand\n$$\\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13.$$\n\nTherefore, the cosine of the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is:\n\n$$\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ -4 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{pmatrix} 12 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}}{5 \\cdot 13} = \\frac{16}{65} = \\frac{8}{65}.$$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\frac{8}{65}$. I hope it is correct."
